Hi,

I think a vnode performs the equivalent function to an inode, in the
top layer of the file system (Called the 'virtual file system', or similar,
it hides the nature of the underlying file system from the user's point of view).

Therefore, an 'out of vnodes' message holds the same significance as an 'out of
inodes' - ie the file system has run out of file identifiers. Do you have an
application that's generating loads of junk files? Maybe you could try changing
one of the file system parameters by doing a 'newfs -i smallnumber' - see the
manpage newfs(8).

  _Dave_

