We're running DEC OSF/1 V1.3 (Rev. 111) on a DEC 3000/600, and find in
a csh script, referring to the argument list as "$*" pollutes the
arguments in a way I don't understand--the pollution has to do with
whether or not backslashes and quotes are expanded in varius
contexts.  The pollution does not occur when the supposedly equivalent
"$argv[*]" is used.  Consider the following shell script "zzz":

#!/bin/csh -f
set var1="$1"
set var2="$argv[*]"
set var3="$1"
set var4="$*"
set var5="$1"
set
echo $var1 $var2 $var3 $var4 $var5
eval "$var1"
eval "$var2"
eval "$var3"
eval "$var4"
eval "$var5"

With the command line

zzz "a'b'\c"

the output (minus the irrelevant part of the output from the "set"
command) is:

var1    a'b'\c
var2    a'b'\c
var3    a'b'\c
var4    a'b'\c
var5    a'b'\c
a'b'\c a'b'\c a'b'\c abc abc
abc: Command not found.
abc: Command not found.
abc: Command not found.
a'b'\c: Command not found.
a'b'\c: Command not found.

Whatever the difference between variables 1-3 (defined before "$*" is
used) and variables 4-5 (defined in terms of or after "$*"), it
doesn't show up in the output of "set", but when the variables are
used by echo or eval it becomes clear there is some difference.  I
don't have this problem with tcsh or with any other brand of csh I've
tried.

Obviously I can work around this using "$argv[*]", just thought
someone out there should know...
-- 
Brian R. Hunt
bhunt@glue.umd.edu


