In article <5HNTBEQG@gwdu03.gwdg.de> bgelbe@gwdu03.gwdg.de (Bodo Gelbe) writes:
>Does anybody know what parameter in the kernel I've to change
>to increase the maximum queue length for packet filter files
>(seems to be 32 in OSF/1 V3.0).

You should be able to do this without hacking the kernel, using
"pfconfig -b 255 -a" (see "man pfconfig").  My /etc/inittab includes
this line

pfconfig:3:wait:/usr/sbin/pfconfig +p +c -a < /dev/null > /dev/console 2>&1

and you should be able to set the backlog at the same time.

Note that some older versions of DEC OSF/1 (and all versions of ULTRIX,
as far as I know) had a bug (oops, my fault!) that prevented non-root
processes from using a backlog of > 32, even if pfconfig -b had been
done to allow a larger one.  But in V3.0, which you have, I'm pretty
sure this bug is fixed.

-Jeff


