The RFC for NFS specifies that the procedures are synchronous.
When a WRITE returns, the client can assume that the data is safe 
and in stable storage.  DEC OSF/1 assumes this means that the data
is on disk or in non-volative memory (Prestoserve).  Some vendors
allow a server to be setup so that a WRITE can return while the data
is still in volatile memory.  This improves the write performance and
the time to copy directory to directory on remote disks.

I don't know that I would expect any server to be faster that a local
copy on a PC unless the PC is very old and slow like mine :-)


