In article <609422082wnr@stek.demon.co.uk>, Gary@stek.demon.co.uk (Gary Whitehead) writes:

>My OSF/1 machine has just gone bezerk,stopping me from forking shells, 
>and terminating tasks. The console is printing up message after 
>message saying 'vnode table is full'.
>
>I have grep'd the man pages but can find no reference to a 'vnode'.

A vnode is a virtual inode; a kernel data structure used to handle
many different file system types.  In early version of OSF/1 there
is a fixed size array of vnodes.  The size of the array is dependent
on the value of maxusers in the configuration file.  Every open
UFS file, NFS file handle and a few other things use vnodes.

The size of the table is set in param.c.  On V1.3 the size
of the table is (NPROC + (2*MAXUSERS) + 128).  If the proceses
you have open enough files you may want to adjust the algorithm.
Or just increase MAXUSERS and rebuild.
  

