Browsing through the blurb for OSF/1 v3.0 it seems that NFS Version 3
includes support for asynchronous writes (and files > 2 GB). 
The bad news is that you need both the server and client to be running 
NFS version 3 to get the new features :-(

We've been using NFS writes on HP workstations on an FDDI LAN for some time. 
OK, maybe it's a kludge, but it removes the bottleneck (the maximum sustained 
write speed is the speed of the disk - a factor 5 or so improvement) and it 
works fine with any NFS client you care to mention. 

While I'm griping about OSF, the default setup for the buffer cache on 
OSF is totally braindead. If you do a lot of I/O the UBC will eat all
the memory on the system, swap all your processes out and grind the
system to a shuddering halt. 
On a 3000/400 with 64 MB RAM modifying the following kernel config
values 
  ubcmaxpercent      40      (default 100)
  ubcseqstartpercent 20      (default 50)
improves things a lot. See the "System  Tuning and Performance Management"
manual for details on this.
                           









