In article <CxxD8t.9E0@bri.hp.com> David McDougall, davidm@bri.hp.com writes:
>Hi Folks,
>          I have a program that happily compiles
>using the standard c compiler on a DEC 3000/300
>running OSF/1 v1.3. It uses a few ioctl calls to
>do various things. I have ported the code to C++
>and I am getting a ridiculous error which states
>the following:
>
>"dec_interface.C:44: error: In this statement, "ioctl" is not declared."
>
>Now line 44 is identical in syntax to the c version and I
>have included the #include <sys/ioctl.h> line in my c++ header
>file. Does anyone know whats wrong or had a similar problem.
>Things are getting silly as there is nothing obviously wrong.
>Famous last words. Are there any defines I should be setting for the
>compiler command.

Make sure that you put 

extern "C" {
}

around all your system includes for C commands. Remember that C defaults
a function to int, but c++ has no defaults, so EVERY function must be 
explicitly declared. In compiling my MUD in DEC c++, I even had to declare
a couple of system functions (select for example) in my own header files,
because they were int functions, and for some reason weren't externed in
the system includes.

It will surely work if you do the extern "C" {}, and if you have to 
declare the function in your own header files.


