Subject: Kernel memory leak found in OSF/1 v3.0
Message-ID: <RAY.BELLIS.94Oct21090013@axp01.mrc-bbc.ox.ac.uk>
Organization: O.U. Dept. of Experimental Psychology
Date: 21 Oct 1994 08:00:13 GMT
Lines: 64

I've found out that there is a memory leak in the /proc file system
handling under OSF/1 v3.0.  I've reported the problem to my CSC who
should hopefully have a fix to me fairly soon.

The leak occurs whenever a process opens an entry in /proc for any
process other than itself.  Each time this happens the kernel allocates
512 bytes of memory for itself and adds it to the TEMP pool.

The reason I was particularly susceptible to this leak is that we use
the `autonice' daemon that I wrote.  Once a minute, the daemon opens
every file in /proc.  On a typical system here with 80 processes running,
this bumps up the kernel size by 40 kB per minute, or 2.4 MB per hour.
On a 32 MB machine it doesn't take that long before all memory is
exhausted, at which point the kernel fails to allocate an `mbuf' and
the system falls over *without rebooting*.

Anyway, here's a simple program which demonstrates the bug.

--8<--8<--
/*
 * memleak.c for OSF/1 v3.0 and /proc fs
 * R.P.Bellis 1994
 */

#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>

/* ARGSUSED */
int main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
	FILE		*fp;
	char		 path[80];

	/* Get path name for *parent* process */
	sprintf(path, "/proc/%05d", getppid());

	/* Open the file */
	if ((fp = fopen(path, "r+")) == 0) {
		perror("fopen");
		return EXIT_FAILURE;
	}

	/* Close the file */
	fclose(fp);

	return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
--8<--8<--

Compile the program, and each time you run it, you'll see the TEMP pool
size increase by 512 bytes (from `vmstat -M').

Ray.
