>for swap space utilization using swapon -s, I see that only
>15% of the swap space is really in use, and the rest is free.
>Can anyone tell me why I still get the message.  I think the
>rule of the thumb is that the swap space in Mb should be atleast
>twice the available memory on the system.  Could this be the 
>reason why the system is complaining? Any input on this problem
>will be appreciated.

>Thanx.

Remove the file /sbin/swapdefault (actually a link to the swap-device). 
Then memory will first be allocated when a programs really needs it (deferred 
swapping).
When the file exists all requested memory will be allocated whether it's 
needed (in use) by a program or not.

Jens
 
