My project is currently investigating possible solutions for providing
access to a CD-rom juke box for the UK Astronomical community.

The specification is still at an early stage but the sort of service to
be provided is:-

1. A server to host a ~230 slot 4-drive CDrom jukebox.

2. Software solution to manage the jukebox and allow access (locally and
from remote clients) to the CDroms "as if they were part of the Unix
filestore".


Ideally we would like our remote sites to be able to mount up the
Jukebox contents as a logical volume i.e. a single mount point
under which appear all the CDrom as if they were all mounted:

                           mnt-pt
                             |
              +----+----+----+----+--...--+--------+
              |    |    |    |    |       |        |
             cd1  cd2  cd3  cd4  cd5 ... cd(N-1)  cdN 

Thus I can see two initial problems. How do we provide transparent
access to the CDs on the server, and how can we provide access to our
clients over the network?

I also see that having such free access to the disks could be
catastrophic if a few users decided to search all the disks for a
certain file!!

I'm sure there are many vendors willing to sell us the server software,
and I'm sure they would love to sell us some client software to access
the archive. However we are restricted by cost. We will have some 200+
machines country-wide that require access to the archive, so
purchasing/licensing client software is out (depending on cost!).

Assuming that we can make the jukebox appear as N ufs/cdfs mounted
disks, we cannot just export a single NFS mount point. Exporting the N
individual mount points is possible but would require a lot of work on
our behalf and that of the remote managers. However, this would be the
cheapest solution!


The server hardware being envisaged is a) an Alpha 3000/300 running
OSF/1 (V3.0 and future versions) , b) a SUN SPARC 10/51 running SOLARIS
V2.3 (and future versions) or c) a PC (DOS - couldn't care less which
version ;-)

Personally I prefer a) as I am the Alpha manager for the project,
however vendors I have spoken to in the past seem to have OSF/1 as the
last o/s on their list of supported architectures. b) suffers from the
same (though less marked) problem (although it would no longer be MY
problem). c) has the advantage of being completely standalone  and
means we can keep the o/s version frozen if needbe. However, I don't
know if the software exists to do what we want on a PC.


There must be some people out there who have set up a  similar service
and who can reccommend the way to procede (or not to procede ;)

If you have any suggestions, can you mail me (with details of hardware/sw
etc) and I'll summarise if their is enough interest. UK contact
addresses for suppliers would be appreciated.

Thanks,

Dave Rawlinson
