In article <37etm9$f7b@paperboy.gsfc.nasa.gov>, jmasino@gtpitch.gsfc.nasa.gov (Jay Masino) writes:
|>
|>I just finished updating my Alpha 7000 from V1.3 to V2.1 (we haven't
|>received 3.0), and everything seemed to go well.  I used the upgrade
|>install, and didn't have any problems.  I was about to go home, when
|>I remembered that I hadn't yet run the "updadmin" command to remove
|>obsolete files.  I started with having it tar/compress the obsolete
|>files, then I had it delete them.  THE SYSTEM WENT NUTS!  It apparently
|>deleted an important library, because every command (even ls) would
|>barf some error message about missing libc.so.  I was able to boot off
|>a diifferent partition that still had our 1.3 image on it, and mount
|>the new filesystem.  I restored the tar/compress file of "obsolete" files
|>and rebooted off the new partition again and everything was OK again.
|>How can I get the obsolete files off now, without risking deleting an
|>important file again!!
|>

If you really tried to update from V1.3 to V2.1, you made a boo-boo as there is
no direct update path between those versions.  Instead, you should have gone from V1.3 to V2.0.  Then from V2.0 to V2.1 using the method documented in the Release Notes, not using "installupdate".

The "Release Notes" for V2.1 document this.  We're looking into implementing a
mechanism which will prevent users from improperly updating new releases of
DEC OSF/1.

V2.1 is a maintenance release for V2.0.  That means it consists of only those
things that changed from V2.0.  On its own, it is only a partial release.  What
this means to you is that you have a V1.3 system with some V2.1 components.  You
don't want this and I haven't any idea what unusual behaviors you may be in for.

The generation of the list of obsolete files comes from comparing the inventory
of the new release with that of the old.  Things in the old that aren't in the
new inventory are considered obsolete.  Since V2.1 is only a partial release, 
it is invalid to compare its inventory to V1.3 (or V2.0, for that matter).  The
resulting list will contain every file that didn't have a maintenance fix for
V2.1.  That's most of DEC OSF/1.  You problably lost lots of commands, libraries, etc when you ran updadmin.

What can you do?  If you can restore your system to V1.3, you can then take the
correct update path to V2.1.  Your current system is in an undefined condition
so I don't know if you could try to "update" it to v2.0 and then to v2.1.  If 
you can't restore to v1.3, I recommend a full install to V2.0 and then update to
V2.1 (please refer to the Release Notes for instructions!)

You should have received V3.0 by now.  If so, I'd recommend you simply do a full
install of that version.

Dave


