back from it's DAT drive!

There have been previous threads that showed the use of 10k block size 
for the dat drive, /dev/rmt0l.  This doesn't make sense?  DAT drives
usually have block sizes in multiples of 2, 1024, 2048 etc. 

Imperically I found that block size of 4k allowed my to write and read
the 2Gb native capacity to /dev/rmt0l.  I.E. 
dd obs=4k of=/dev/rmt0l if=/dev/null

If you try obs=10k you end up writing less than 2Gb.

You verify where the EOF marker is written by reversing the process:

dd ibs=4k if=/dev/rmt0l of=/dev/null

********

