Although I've found a kludgy workaround (see below), I'd like
to describe a problem we've been seeing on our Sable systems,
and find out whether anyone else has seen it, and whether it's
known whether the problem is flaky hardware, an OSF/1 bug, or
whatever.  (N.b.: If it's flaky hardware, then it's probably a
flaky *design* rather than a sick machine, since we've seen the
problem on both our systems.)

The problem is that periodically, and for no apparent reason,
all inbound ethernet packets get stuck somewhere (I say "stuck",
rather than "lost", intentionally; see below).  From the outside,
the machine seems dead or off the network; attempts to ping it
produce no response.

However, if I login at the console, and ping some other system,
not only do all the outgoing ping packets arrive successfully at
their intended destination, but the act of sending them appears
to successfully unclog everything in the inbound direction.  One
does see a delay of (possibly) several seconds, with results such
as the following:

seal> ping walrus
PING walrus.cita (128.100.76.59): 56 data bytes
64 bytes from 128.100.76.59: icmp_seq=0 ttl=255 time=4366 ms
64 bytes from 128.100.76.59: icmp_seq=1 ttl=255 time=3367 ms
64 bytes from 128.100.76.59: icmp_seq=2 ttl=255 time=2369 ms
64 bytes from 128.100.76.59: icmp_seq=3 ttl=255 time=1372 ms
64 bytes from 128.100.76.59: icmp_seq=4 ttl=255 time=374 ms
64 bytes from 128.100.76.59: icmp_seq=5 ttl=255 time=0 ms
64 bytes from 128.100.76.59: icmp_seq=7 ttl=255 time=1 ms
64 bytes from 128.100.76.59: icmp_seq=8 ttl=255 time=2 ms
64 bytes from 128.100.76.59: icmp_seq=9 ttl=255 time=2 ms
64 bytes from 128.100.76.59: icmp_seq=10 ttl=255 time=0 ms
64 bytes from 128.100.76.59: icmp_seq=11 ttl=255 time=0 ms

Note the really huge "time=" values on the first several lines
of output.  Also, the reason I think that inbound packets have
simply been stuck somewhere rather than lost is that stuff which
had been sent to that system while it was in constipation mode
does seem to get through successfully once things have unclogged.
For example, if I've logged in to one of these systems from my own
workstation, and have tried typing <cr> to the shell a bunch of
times (and getting no response, of course), then as soon as I've
unclogged things via the "ping something" kludge, I'll suddenly
get N consecutive shell prompts in response to the N*<cr> which
I'd sent.

So my "fix" is to ensure that there's a shell script running which
just pings some other host every five seconds.  Since doing that,
we haven't seen the problem recur even once.

Some other possibly relevant data:  (1) We're running OSF/1 V2.0b;
if anyone has in fact seen this problem, I'd be interested to hear
whether V3 fixes it.  (2) While I don't have any hard statistical
evidence, it nonetheless appears that the problem occurs much more
frequently when there's lots of ethernet traffic, and in particular,
when the collision rate is high.

