If you're using latex (which I think the original poster is), you might
consider grabbing the latex style file package called psfrag.  Psfrag allows
one to take postscript code with embedded "labels" and translate them to
suitable latex code, which can then be tex'd etc.

I use this all the time with gnuplot.  Say I want to place a complicated
equation somewhere on my plot.  Using gnuplot, I generate my plot and put a
label (like "e1") at the place in the plot I want the equation to appear.
Generate the postscript (or eps).  In your latex document, you pull in the
postscript output and detail what translation occurs: (I'm doing this from my
head-- get the psfrag doc for correct syntazx, etc.)

	\psfrag {e1}[][]{$\left \lceil \frac {\alpha^2}{\mu} \right \rceil}

I've used this approach for several papers and my thesis and is very
convienent (IMO).  

One advantage of this approach is that I can fine tune everything from within
latex.  If I placed my label at the wrong spot, I add \hspace and \vspace in
my latex code to put the equation at the location I want.  

Brian


