NNTP-Posting-Host: zeus.maths.monash.edu.au
Keywords: FORTRAN, alpha, complex

I have found a very nasty bug in the DEC FORTRAN compiler for AXP (alpha)
machines. Exponetiation of a complex*16 number gives rubbish. Here is an
example in which the square root of (4.d0,0.d0) is calculated in three
different ways:-


	program tst

	complex*16 s,sr_power,sr_exp,sr_sqrt

	s = (4.d0,0.d0)

	sr_power = s**0.5
	sr_exp   = exp(0.5*cdlog(s))
	sr_sqrt  = sqrt(s)

	write(6,1)s,sr_power,sr_exp,sr_sqrt

	stop
 1	format('s                 =',d14.7,x,d14.7,/,
     &	       's**0.5            =',d14.7,x,d14.7,/,
     &	       'exp(0.5*cdlog(s)) =',d14.7,x,d14.7,/,
     &	       'sqrt(s)           =',d14.7,x,d14.7)
	end


-- and here is the output:

s                 = 0.4000000D+01  0.0000000D+00
s**0.5            = 0.1284025D+01  0.0000000D+00
exp(0.5*cdlog(s)) = 0.2000000D+01  0.0000000D+00
sqrt(s)           = 0.2000000D+01  0.0000000D+00

As you can see, s**0.5 gets it wildly wrong. The other methods work.
The strange thing is that I would have thought that s**0.5 actually
worked by evaluating exp(0.5*cdlog(s)).

I was running DEC Fortran V3.4 (DFABASE340) with DFARTL342 DEC Fortran
for OSF/1 AXP Runtime Support under OSF/1 2.0B on a DEC 3000/800.

Is this a known bug? Has it been/is it being fixed?

