>> I rlogin to HP, DEC Alpha OSF1, Sun systems. I have had
>> some script in .login which would set the DISPLAY variable.
>> But it uses the 'whoami' command to find out from which
>> system I have logged in. Unfortunately the output of
>> whomai is not the same on all these systems. Was wondering
>> if you folks have some script which would set the DISPLAY
>> environment variable correctly on ALL systems.

>	Stefan

>PS: ultimately, the best solution is to telnet instead of rlogin and
>hope the telnets are recent enough to include the "last" extensions:
>this allows you to pass any env var to the remote host (as is always
>done for TERM). This way, the DISPLAY var is kept correct even if you
>log from X to Y to Z (in which case the "who" thing would try to
>display on Y)

or try out this:

#!/bin/sh
#
#	erlogin - extended rlogin that propogates DISPLAY environment var
#
# 24-mar-1994 jrv add support for console DISPLAYs and {} env var wrappers
# 24-feb-1994 jrv exec rlogin instead of just calling it (frees proc slot)
# 23-feb-1994 jrv add csh (C shell) support
# 15-feb-1994 jrv initial release <John_Vanderpool@gsfc.nasa.gov> (fish)
#
# NOTES:
#	telnet does this for you but rlogin needs to adopt the same philosophy
#
#	this relies on the simple fact that rlogin does pass the TERM env var
#
#	the rlogin protocol gives you a "Terminal type too long" error after
#	len($TERM) > ~50 chars, so if you have an extremely long hostname (or
#	you use csh) you may have to resort to using IP numbers.
#
#	recommended installation directory is /usr/local/bin/erlogin.
#
#	you must have the following in any $HOME/.profile (or $HOME/.login for 
#	csh users) on the rlogin target (remote) host (server/rlogind) side,
#	or have your sysadmin install in /etc/profile (or /etc/cshrc [or
#	/etc/csh.login on some boxes] for csh).
#
# special setup for [k]sh erlogin (extended rlogin) to pass DISPLAY variable
# echo "${TERM:-}" | grep "DISPLAY=" >/dev/null 2>&1
# if [ $? = 0 ]; then
#   eval "${TERM:-}"
#   export TERM DISPLAY
# fi
#
# special setup for csh erlogin (extended rlogin) to pass DISPLAY variable
# echo "$TERM" | grep "DISPLAY" >&! /dev/null
# if ($status == 0) eval "$TERM"


## I wanted to extend this to pass any env vars you want but the "Terminal type
## too long" error (see above) blows that, dang!
##ENVLIST="DISPLAY"		# list any additional env vars you want to pass
##
##TERM="__ERLOGIN__ TERM=$TERM; export TERM"
##
##for env in $ENVLIST
##do
##  NEXT=`eval echo $"$env"`
##  TERM="$TERM; $env=$NEXT; export $env"
##done


RDISPLAY=${DISPLAY:-}

case ${DISPLAY:-nodisplay} in		# special handling for console
  :[0-9]* | unix:[0-9]* | localhost:[0-9]* )
    display=`echo ${DISPLAY:-} | cut -f2 -d: | cut -f1 -d.`
    screen=`echo ${DISPLAY:-} | cut -f2 -d: | cut -f2 -d.`
    if [ "${screen:-}" != "" ]; then screen=".$screen"; fi
    RDISPLAY="`hostname`:${display}${screen:-}"
  ;;
esac


# this ASS-U-MEs $SHELL is set (correctly) and is the same on both sides

if [ "`basename ${SHELL:-}`" = "csh" ]; then
  TERM="setenv TERM $TERM;setenv DISPLAY $RDISPLAY"

else	# assume ksh, sh, bash style
  TERM="TERM=$TERM;DISPLAY=${RDISPLAY:-}"
fi

export TERM


# intent of the following was going to allow the real rlogin to be renamed
# rlogin.real and then have erlogin installed as rlogin; however, rlogin
# apparently checks that argv[0] is "rlogin" - oh well...

##if [ -x `type rlogin.real | cut -f3 -d" "` ]; then
##  exec rlogin.real $*
##else
  exec rlogin $*
##fi
--
