Simplified License Pricing

Alpha AXP OSF/1 and Alpha AXP OpenVMS software products are priced to be 
competitive with similar offerings on UNIX platforms -- specifically HP-UX, 
Sun's Solaris, and IBM's AIX systems. Fewer part numbers and prices make it 
easier to compare Digital pricing with similar competitive offers.

Simplified License Types

Previously Digital offered two types of capacity based licenses and two 
types of user based licenses. Users found it difficult to evaluate the 
differences between license types and multiple price points. Now, 
Traditional Licenses are based on three simple system classes. ClusterWide 
Licenses will no longer be offered for new software purchases.

The popular Concurrent Use License offering is now available on many 
compilers, and will be Digital's primary user based license type. Personal 
Use Licenses will continue to be orderable on products that already offer 
them.

Simplified License Upgrade and Migration Policy

Digital will provide users with a consistent algorithm for protecting their 
software license investments. When upgrading or migrating software, users 
will receive a 75 percent credit for the license being traded in toward the 
purchase of the new license for the same product. (The maximum credit 
provided will not exceed 75 percent of the new license price.) This policy 
applies to VAX, Alpha AXP, and MIPS systems, including:

o  Upgrades to a higher system class (e.g., Departmental to Enterprise).
o  Migrations across architectures (e.g., VAX to Alpha AXP systems).
o  Migrations between operating system platforms (e.g., OpenVMS to OSF/1 
   systems).
o  Migrations from ClusterWide Licenses to Traditional Licenses.
o  Migrations from licenses based on tiers to licenses based on system 
   classes.
o  Migrations between user based licenses (e.g., Personal to Concurrent).

To calculate the credit received for trading in a license:

1. Compare the price of:
   a. The license being traded in.
   b. The license being purchased.

2. Calculate credit as 75 percent of the lower price.

3. Subtract credit from the purchase price of the new license.

NOTE: User discount agreements apply to credit and final price.

BENEFITS

These fundamental changes to Digital's license structure, pricing, and 
upgrade policies reduce the software cost of ownership. Reducing the number 
of times a license upgrade transaction is required reduces incremental fees, 
and time spent with paperwork. Flexibility to move licenses over a broader 
range of systems allows IS managers to be more responsive to demands of 
their end users.

AVAILABILITY AND ORDERING

Pricing for software licensing based on the new system class structure for 
