In article <36bj0n$sh1@niaomi.iscm.ulst.ac.uk>, yufan@iscm.ulst.ac.uk (Yufan Hu) writes:
>I would like to know whether the DAT tapes on DEC Alpha running OSF1 will be 
>readable from a HP machine. I heard that DEC uses a special compression 
>method in the tape driver. If it is true, how can I turn this compression 
>off?

I have yet to find a DAT drive that does not conform to the DDS
standard.  What this means is that I have been able to read any
DAT written on any one of our machines (including HP and DEC) on
any other one of our machines.

If you're using a TLZ06 on the DEC machine and the DAT drive on
your HP is not capable of compression, then you'll want to use
the "l" device name on the DEC.  (e.g. if the DAT is rmt0, you'll
want to use /dev/rmt0l)

