mr@stek.demon.co.uk (Mike Rawlings) writes:

>I would appreciate any information about memory usage on DEC alpha 
>systems running OSF-1.

>My system is a DEC 300L with 32M RAM and 128M swap space, 

...

>Increasing the memory to 48M or 64M certainly improves matters, increasing the 
>swap space prevents the swap space warnings but makes little difference to the performance.   

>Am I being unrealistic expecting this system to perform in 32M RAM, or 
>are there any ways of 'tuning' OSF-1 to reduce memory use?   I would 
>appreciate any comments on using shared libraries for the application code, 
>or on tuning SoftWindows performance.


"unrealistic" yes 	"'tuning'" yes

If you only have 32 meg and 128 meg of swap then the total of the two
is the ultimate limit of how much you can run at once. You can improve
matters prior to that point by rm'ing /sbin/swapdefault. This will allow
the system to use the deferred mode for allocating swap space, thus only
allocating space as needed. Something else that you can do is to change
MAXUSERS from the default 32 down to 16 (or even 8) in your configuration
file and rebuilding your kernel. (see the man page for doconfig)

I have recently acquired a 300LX and almost immediately upgraded to 48MEG.
Even though I had enough swap space, the performance was dog-meat and the
disk drive sounded like a chipmunk with all of the paging/swapping. As you
can see below , I also setup a lot more swap space than you did.

koibito.iisc.com> swapon -s
Swap partition /dev/rz3b (default swap):
    Allocated space:        19947 pages (155MB)
    In-use space:            4128 pages ( 20%)
    Free space:             15819 pages ( 79%)

Swap partition /dev/rz0b:
    Allocated space:        16384 pages (128MB)
