>Is that any SCSI harddisk can be attached to our system and we only need
>the informations to filled in the /etc/disktab files?

Any well behaved SCSI disk should work, though it is always wise
to check with the vendor first.  When a SCSI device *is* well
behaved you don't a disktab entry either.  The disklabel command
can query the drive to get the geometry information and write
the default partition table.  You can then modify the partition
table to taste.  Disklabel won't be able to get the rotational
speed, so that will need to be changed by editing the disk label
is the information is important.
 
