>What hardware was the system running on ?  What sorts of things
>were the users doing ?  What was the performance like ?

Well, I have a year-old copy of the AIM benchmark result book showing 
a 3000-500X maxed at 805 simulated users.  The system had 1*rz26 and
3*rz25 disks and 256 MB.  The quoted price for that system was
$98,920.  I'm sure if you surf around gatekeeper.dec.com
(www.digital.com) you can find the 'performance briefs' to give you
more recent info.

AIM is a kinda small workload (the procs never call brk() so they stay
the same size, plus or minus stack use) but they are all quite active
(no artificial calls to sleep() to simulate think time).  Each 'user'
can be one or two processes (the second one is created to test pipe
performance).  They do things like math, disk IO, string functions,
etc. - it's a synthetic workload.

As for real users, fortunately most of the Alphas I use don't have too
many on them at any one time :-)

