Under OSF/1 3.0, I've been looking at the file-on-file filesystem.
Mounting files on top of files works OK (i.e. "mount -t ffm foo bar"
for files foo and bar) but trying to mount a directory onto a
mount point (file or directory) fails. For example, given a directory
foo and a file bar as mount point:
    % cd /tmp
    % ls -ld foo bar
    -rw-r--r--   1 mbeattie xzdg           0 Oct  5 16:58 bar
    drwxr-xr-x   2 mbeattie xzdg         512 Oct  5 16:57 foo
trying to use mount(8) to mount foo on bar gives:
    % mount -t ffm foo bar
    foo on /tmp/bar: No valid filesystem exists on this partition

In case this was a problem with mount(8) and not mount(2), I coded
up a little C program using mount(2) and the mount attempt returns
EINVAL (whether the mount point bar is a file or a directory).
Now the man page for mount(2) says:
    For file-on-file mounting, the mount() function mounts a file specified by
    the data parameter onto another file specified by the mnt-path parameter.
    Either file may be of any type, but  mnt-path cannot already have a file
    system or another file mounted on it.
The phrase "Either file may be of any type" suggests to me that mounting
a directory should work, unless it's just referring to ordinay files,
device specials and so on (which *do* work OK). Does anyone know
whether this should/will work?

